        "thinking": "So `test-embedded-postgres.ts` uses `await import(\"embedded-postgres\")` - a dynamic import. This means esbuild WILL try to bundle it.\n\nAnd I can see that `embedded-postgres-error.ts` does NOT import `embedded-postgres` directly (it's a pure utility).\n\nSo the only file that directly imports `embedded-postgres` is `test-embedded-postgres.ts` via a dynamic import.\n\nThe question is: why does esbuild fail on this? Dynamic imports with `await import(...)` should be treated as code-splitting points, and if the imported module (embedded-postgres) can't be found, it should just throw at runtime.\n \nWait, I see it now - the error is about `@embedded-postgres/darwin-x64` and other platform-specific binaries that `embedded-postgres` tries to dynamically import. esbuild is attempting to bundle all of these optional native dependencies, but they're not available for the current platform. The solution is to mark these platform-specific packages as external in the esbuild configuration so they're not bundled.\n\nLooking at wrangler's build options, I can use a custom build command in the `[build]` section of wrangler.toml to pass the `--external` flag to esbuild, or I might be able to configure this through wrangler's own build settings. Let me explore what configuration options are actually available here.\n\nActually, I just realized a much simpler solution: the main index file is importing from a test file that dynamically requires `embedded-postgres`, so if I move those test exports to a separate subpath, the bundler won't try to include that problematic module at all.\n\nNow I need to check which server or service files are actually using those test database functions.",
